From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fexiven <48439988+Fexiven@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 10:42:54 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 12/23] rocket: rk3576: batch jobs and split layers over both
 NPU cores

Every task was its own job, and a job costs about 50 us of scheduler,
interrupt and IOMMU overhead against 20-50 us of NPU work per task. One
process also never used the second core.

 - Job batching: the tasks of consecutive operations go into one job per
   core; a job is only cut where a task needs rows that the other core
   computes. ROCKET_BATCH=0 submits one job per task.
 - Two-core region split, used when the kernel can pin jobs to a core
   (DRM_ROCKET_PARAM_NUM_CORES >= 2, drm_rocket_job.core): rkt_mc_plan
   walks the subgraph backwards and gives every splittable operation an
   output row range per core, its own half plus the halo rows its
   consumers on that core read, so chains of layers run on both cores
   without cross-core waits. Where the recomputed rows would cost too
   much the operation falls back to plain halves and the jobs are cut
   there. Core 1 uses the vendor's single-core core-1 CBUF window (banks
   7..13), so the cores never share banks. Element-wise ADD tasks are
   split per 16-channel surface and row range. ROCKET_MULTICORE=0 keeps
   everything on core 0.
 - Output conversion walks the destination sequentially with the int8
   flip folded in; a BO is mapped once and the mapping kept.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
